<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>Introducing the Zero Product Property</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m02141</md:content-id>
    <md:title>Introducing the Zero Product Property</md:title>
    <md:uuid>e9d7613a-4686-419d-8d71-55a3dab73aaf</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
    <section class="numbered-exercises" id="sect-00001"><title>Warm Up</title>
      <para id="para-00001">What values of the variables make each equation true?</para>
      <exercise id="_8ad43010-4e4f-445c-9428-d077659343f1" class="multiple-choice">
        <problem id="prob-00001">
          <para id="para-00002">Enter the value of a.</para>
          <para id="para-00003"><m:math><m:mrow><m:mn>6</m:mn><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mn>2</m:mn><m:mi>a</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>0</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math></para></problem>
        <solution id="sol-00001">
          <para id="para-00004"><m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>–</m:mi><m:mn>3</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math></para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <exercise id="_5a986493-88af-432e-8330-00139cfbe17e" class="multiple-choice">
        <problem id="prob-00002">
          <para id="para-00006">Enter the value of b.</para>
          <para id="para-00007"><m:math><m:mrow><m:mn>7</m:mn><m:mi>b</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>0</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math></para></problem>
        <solution id="sol-00002">
          <para id="para-00008"><m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mn>0</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math></para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <exercise id="_81cbe54c-941e-4369-a118-64ef330c2b6c" class="multiple-choice">
        <problem id="prob-00003">
          <para id="para-00010">Enter the value of c.</para>
          <para id="para-00011"><m:math><m:mrow><m:mn>7</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mi>c</m:mi><m:mi>−</m:mi><m:mn>5</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>0</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math></para></problem>
        <solution id="sol-00003">
          <para id="para-00012"><m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mn>3</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math></para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <exercise id="_3c031f12-8a5b-4bc4-b1f4-bffffb2e1cd5" class="multiple-choice">
        <problem id="prob-00004">
          <para id="para-00014">Determine which of the statements are true if <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>g</m:mi><m:mi>·</m:mi><m:mi>h</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>0</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math>. HINT: There may be more than one statement that is true.</para>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00004">
          <para id="para-00015">(g=0\), <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>h</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>0</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math>, and <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>g</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mi>h</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>0</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math>. Either <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>g</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>0</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math> or <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>h</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>0</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math>. But also, notice, if <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>g</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>0</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math> and <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>h</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>0</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math> at the same time, you can also say that <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>g</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mi>h</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>0</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math>.</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <exercise id="exer-00001">
        <problem id="prob-00005">
          <para id="para-00017">What did you notice about one of the factors in each of the previous problems?</para>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00005">
          <para id="para-00019">Compare your answer: </para>
          <para id="para-00020">At least one of the factors in each equation was 0 or equivalent to 0.</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction End -->
      <para id="para-00021">We will use the <term class="glossary-tooltip" id="term-00001">zero product property</term> to help solve <term class="glossary-tooltip" id="term-00002">quadratic equations</term> throughout this lesson.</para>
      <note id="note-00001">
        <title>ZERO PRODUCT PROPERTY</title>
  <para id="para-00022">If the product of two expressions is 0, then one or both of the expressions equal 0.</para>
  <para id="para-00023">If <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>a</m:mi><m:mi>b</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>0</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math>, then <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>a</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>0</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math> or <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>b</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>0</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math>, or <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>a</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mi>b</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>0</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math>.</para>
      </note>
    </section>
  </content>
</document>